content.text:
I reviewed the eight target files directly. The repo is a git repo, but every one of them is untracked, so there was no diff to work from. Findings 1 to 3 are the most likely to cost results:

1. Resuming can be refused for good because the run fingerprint covers every parquet file in the input folder.
2. A video whose inference fails is left out of the output while the run still exits 0.
3. A failed listing at startup can let a new run delete an older checkpoint.

No CLAUDE.md rule is clearly broken. Nothing was run or verified, as the review recipe specifies. Finding 8 relies on my knowledge that the `hf` command first shipped in huggingface_hub 0.34; I couldn't confirm it because the local `.venv` doesn't have that package.

```json
[
  {
    "file": "run.py",
    "line": 219,
    "summary": "The checkpoint fingerprint includes the name and size of every *.parquet in the input folder, even with --limit-videos, so resuming fails whenever the set of downloaded files has changed.",
    "failure_scenario": "colab/SETUP.md step 6 runs `--limit-videos 64` while the full 50-file download runs in the background. Say 12 files exist at the first run. After a runtime reset the user re-runs the download (SETUP step 6) and resumes when 7 or 30 files are present. The fingerprint's `inputs` list differs, so restore() raises CheckpointError, main returns 2, and the run can only start again under a new --run-name (from zero) or after waiting for exactly the original file set."
  },
  {
    "file": "run.py",
    "line": 287,
    "summary": "A video that ends in InferenceError is skipped without any output line, and the run still returns 0 unless 3 failures happen in a row.",
    "failure_scenario": "One very high-resolution video OOMs even at batch size 1 (run_inference raises InferenceError). Its id never goes into the output or the sidecar, the next video succeeds and resets infer_failures, and main returns 0. results.jsonl has no lines and no error line for that video, which breaks 'Damaged video -> one error line'. Every --resume retries it and fails the same way. An intermittent driver fault on every other video drops half of them, again with exit 0."
  },
  {
    "file": "vidx/checkpoint.py",
    "line": 198,
    "summary": "has_existing() treats a listing failure as 'no checkpoint', so a fresh run passes the overwrite guard and later deletes the existing checkpoint.",
    "failure_scenario": "The HF API times out (or the token is not yet available) at startup, and the user runs without --resume and with the same --run-name. has_existing logs a warning and returns False, and the run starts. Once the network is back, _snapshot numbers its generations after the old ones, and prune(keep=2) deletes the old run's last generations after two snapshots. The previous run's only durable copy is gone."
  },
  {
    "file": "vidx/checkpoint.py",
    "line": 211,
    "summary": "restore() does not catch exceptions from store.fetch(), so a missing or failed HF generation file aborts the whole restore instead of falling back to an older generation.",
    "failure_scenario": "The newest HF generation is missing manifest.json (a partly pruned or partly uploaded folder), or one hf_hub_download call fails. EntryNotFoundError or a ConnectionError propagates out of restore(), and main prints 'checkpoint: ...' and returns 2. The valid older generation, and the Drive store, are never tried; the 'using an older one' path only runs when validate_generation returns None."
  },
  {
    "file": "vidx/checkpoint.py",
    "line": 100,
    "summary": "DirStore treats a missing root as an empty store, so --resume with Drive not mounted restarts from zero and checkpoints to disk that is wiped on reset.",
    "failure_scenario": "After a reset the user forgets drive.mount() and runs `--checkpoint-dir /content/drive/MyDrive/vidx-ckpt --resume`. The glob over the missing path returns [], restore() reports 'no checkpoint found', and all videos are processed again. publish() then mkdir(parents=True) creates /content/drive/... on the ephemeral local disk, so the next reset loses everything as well."
  },
  {
    "file": "vidx/checkpoint.py",
    "line": 256,
    "summary": "_next is computed by listing all stores in one expression outside the per-store try, so one store's listing failure blocks the snapshot for every store.",
    "failure_scenario": "With --checkpoint-dir and --checkpoint-hf-repo both set, HF is unreachable when the first snapshot runs. HFStore.list_gens raises inside the max(...) call, _snapshot aborts, and the healthy Drive store gets nothing until HF comes back. This contradicts 'a failing store only costs a warning'. A prune() failure after a successful publish is also counted as a failed snapshot, so the frames are not marked as sent and get uploaded again."
  },
  {
    "file": "vidx/output.py",
    "line": 359,
    "summary": "A legacy sidecar entry (no line count) is accepted as done even when none of its lines are in the output, and it is rewritten as 'video\\t0', so the video is never processed again.",
    "failure_scenario": "An older-format sidecar lists 'a:5', but the output lost a:5's lines (the file was truncated or replaced). The comprehension accepts it because n is None, records the count as len(found)=0, and writes 'a:5\\t0'. Every later resume skips a:5, and the final results have no lines for that video. video_lines always returns at least one line, so a count of 0 should never count as done."
  },
  {
    "file": "requirements.txt",
    "line": 12,
    "summary": "huggingface_hub is pinned to 0.29.3, but colab/SETUP.md (lines 57 and 61) and CLAUDE.md use the `hf download` CLI, which first shipped in huggingface_hub 0.34.",
    "failure_scenario": "Following SETUP.md in a fresh 3.11 venv: `pip install -r requirements.txt` installs 0.29.3, which has only `huggingface-cli`. Step 5's `hf download ...` fails with 'hf: command not found', so no data is downloaded, and the nohup background download fails silently into download.log."
  },
  {
    "file": "vidx/checkpoint.py",
    "line": 111,
    "summary": "prune() keeps the newest KEEP generation numbers whether or not they are valid, so a half-published generation can push out the last good fallback.",
    "failure_scenario": "Drive publish of gen 5 fails halfway (no manifest) while the HF store succeeds, so _next becomes 6. Drive gen 6 publishes and prune keeps [5, 6] and deletes gen 4. Only one valid Drive generation is left, and if gen 6 is later found corrupt, restore() has nothing in that store to fall back on."
  },
  {
    "file": "vidx/checkpoint.py",
    "line": 242,
    "summary": "Snapshots run synchronously in the per-video loop. Each one gzips the whole output, re-reads every payload file to hash it, uploads a full copy, and lists the whole HF repo (including all frames/*.npz) twice.",
    "failure_scenario": "On a long run the output reaches tens of MB and frames/ holds thousands of files. Every 10 minutes the GPU sits idle while write_generation compresses and hashes, upload_folder pushes the full archive, and list_gens runs in both _next and prune. Total upload grows quadratically over the run, and the measured compute_s includes this checkpoint overhead. The cheaper option is to hash while writing, keep the generation count in memory, and run the upload in a background thread."
  }
]
```
